        insight: "The immediate crisis is managed conventionally - handover plans, a structured goodbye to regulars with deliberate introductions to two senior colleagues, the product house engaged to accelerate a second assessor. The direction-changing move is what follows: the director treats the near-miss as a free audit of every other key-person exposure and runs the resignation thought-experiment across the whole structure. The findings are uncomfortable - the spa manager holds the rota logic personally, one receptionist is the only fluent user of the membership system, and two of the top five revenue-generating treatments can be delivered by just two people each. Over two quarters the director builds the machinery this module describes: a skills matrix with quarterly training targets (every top-ten treatment deliverable by at least four people), named and genuinely developed deputies for the three critical roles, SOPs for the knowledge that lived in heads, and a progression architecture - two new senior-therapist-with-training-duties roles, at a pay step - that both closed the assessor gap and gave the next generation a reason to stay. Eighteen months later a second senior departure passes almost unnoticed operationally. The director's note to the GM: 'the first resignation cost us a crisis; the design work means the second one cost us a leaving card'.",
